court, old and afraid and tired of being afraid. a daguerreotype in Margaret Ellison's hands loosened their tongue: yes, they had seen. Yes, they would swear. No, they had never dared.\n\nMargaret Ellison wrote it all down, every thread, every name, and sealed it three ways. If the mill owner silenced them, the story would still speak. character is destiny — even in a Cornish village, even then."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': "the mill owner laughed — then didn't. The evidence was a chain, each link forged: a daguerreotype, the register, the witness, the ledger page the maid Annie had risked everything to copy. Link by link, it held.\n\nThe magistrate squirmed. The crowd — a Cornish village's people, mill hands and merchants' wives — began to murmur, and the murmur became a voice. the mill owner's money couldn't buy this room. Not tonight.\n\nMargaret Ellison stood in the emptying hall with a daguerreotype and felt not triumph but tiredness, and under it, peace. character is destiny. It had cost plenty. It was worth it."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Restoration', 'text': "a Cornish village changed, the way places do when a bad tooth is pulled: slowly, then all at once. The mill got new owners. The court got a new magistrate. a daguerreotype got a place of honor — and then, deliberately, a quiet drawer.\n\ncharacter is destiny — Margaret Ellison had it carved, finally, on no monument at all, just lived it, in a Cornish village, in every ordinary, extraordinary day.\n\nYears later, Margaret Ellison would show a daguerreotype to the curious and say: 'This is what courage looks like when it's old and paper.' And the young would listen, and a Cornish village would remember."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Coda: The Drawer', 'text': "character is destiny. In a Cornish village, in every place, in every age. The end — which is to say, the beginning.\n\na daguerreotype rests now in Margaret Ellison's drawer, among the bills and the birthday cards. Sometimes Margaret Ellison takes it out, just to hold the weight of it: proof that one person, in a Cornish village, in any age, can tip the scales.\n\nThe gaslight is electric now. The carriages are cars. But a daguerreotype is still a daguerreotype, and Margaret Ellison is still Margaret Ellison, and the drawer still opens."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()

